# Coverage gate configuration -- the single place to edit when ratcheting.
#
# BASELINE_* is what CI enforces today. It is set to the coverage this repo
# actually has, not to the coverage it wants, so the gate turns green honestly
# while still failing the build the moment a change drops coverage. A gate
# pinned to an aspirational number has to be left disabled, which is exactly
# how this repo ended up reporting "Failing" on every passing build.
#
# TARGET_* is the long-term goal. It is never enforced; it is printed beside
# the baseline in the PR comment so the remaining gap stays visible instead of
# quietly disappearing once the build is green.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How these numbers are derived:  BASELINE = floor(min(observed) - 1.0)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `min(observed)` spans BOTH environments -- local macOS and Linux CI -- not
# just the one you happen to be sitting in front of. Two independent effects
# make a single-run baseline wrong, and the first release of this gate got
# caught by both (see issue #14):
#
# 1. Run-to-run drift. Coverage moves without any source change, because line
#    execution inside passing tests depends on async ordering and timing. Domain
#    was observed at 54.0 and 53.1 on the same machine with identical test
#    outcomes (1310 passed, 572 skipped both times) -- 0.9 points of drift. A
#    baseline set from one run left 0.1 points of headroom and would have turned
#    main red for nobody's mistake.
#
# 2. Platform-level denominator differences. Flutter only instruments libraries
#    the test run loads, and the loaded set was not identical across platforms.
#    lib/shared/widgets/language_switcher.dart (98 lines, 0% covered) was in the
#    local lcov and absent from CI's, which alone moved `shared` from 47.8%
#    local to 60.2% on CI.
#
#    Issue #15 removed that divergence: test/coverage_all_test.dart now imports
#    every eligible library under lib/, so the denominator is derived from the
#    filesystem instead of from whatever the run happened to load, and the two
#    environments measure the same set of files.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The numbers below DROPPED in #15, and that drop is not a regression
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 1112 lines of previously invisible code entered the denominator (15584 ->
# 16696). Nothing got worse; the measurement stopped flattering itself. 65 files
# no test imported were absent from lcov entirely -- not counted as 0%, just
# missing -- and they are now counted. Per the note at the bottom of this file,
# lowering is legitimate exactly here, because the measurement changed meaning.
#
#   layer         before (min)  after (min)  baseline  moved by
#   total         32.3          30.6         29        -1.7
#   domain        53.1          54.0         53        +0.9
#   data          18.9          18.6         17        -0.3
#   presentation  37.0          36.7         35        -0.3
#   core          14.0          11.7         10        -2.3
#   shared        47.8          35.5         34        -12.3
#
# `core` and `shared` fell hardest because that is where the unimported files
# were concentrated -- lib/core/performance/, lib/core/utils/ and the shared
# widgets. `domain` rose slightly: the aggregator loads barrel files whose
# exported libraries were already covered.
#
# Recorded observations behind the values below:
#
#   layer         local (2 runs)  CI       min     baseline
#   total         30.6, 30.6      PENDING  30.6    29
#   domain        54.0, 54.0      PENDING  54.0    53
#   data          18.6, 18.6      PENDING  18.6    17
#   presentation  36.7, 36.7      PENDING  36.7    35
#   core          11.7, 11.7      PENDING  11.7    10
#   shared        35.5, 35.5      PENDING  35.5    34
#
# The two local runs were byte-identical -- no run-to-run drift this time,
# unlike in #14. CI figures are filled in from the run on the pull request that
# introduces this change, before it merges; if CI comes in lower on any layer,
# the baseline moves down to floor(CI - 1.0) and this table records both.
#
# Ratcheting: after tests land and BOTH environments report a higher number,
# raise the matching BASELINE_* here. Nothing else needs to change. Never lower
# a baseline to make a red build green -- that is the regression the gate exists
# to catch. Lowering is legitimate only when a measurement changes meaning, as
# in #15, and then it belongs in that change with the reason recorded.

BASELINE_TOTAL=29
BASELINE_DOMAIN=53
BASELINE_DATA=17
BASELINE_PRESENTATION=35
BASELINE_CORE=10
BASELINE_SHARED=34

TARGET_TOTAL=80
TARGET_DOMAIN=100
TARGET_DATA=90
TARGET_PRESENTATION=80
TARGET_CORE=80
TARGET_SHARED=80
